2. Main features and how they work
1. Multi-role authentication







behance.net


The application supports two types of users:
- Patient: Can register, log in, book appointments and manage medical records.
- Doctor: Can manage appointment requests, accept or reject appointments and mark consultations as completed.
How it works:
1. User registers with their details and role.
2. Backend validates the registration information.
3. User logs in.
4. Backend generates a JWT token.
5. Frontend uses the token for protected API requests.
6. Backend checks authentication and role-based permissions.
Interview concept: JWT authentication and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).
2. Doctor appointment booking







uxpilot.ai


Patients can:
- Search for doctors.
- Select doctors manually.
- Get AI-assisted doctor specialization suggestions.
- View available appointment slots.
- Book appointments.
Doctors can accept, reject or complete appointments.

Real-world example: Two patients book the same slot.

Suppose Dr. Kumar has only one available slot at 10:00 AM.

Patient A and Patient B try booking simultaneously.

The backend should:
1. Check slot availability.
2. Use a database transaction.
3. Ensure only one booking succeeds.
4. Return a booking conflict to the other patient.
This is an important interview topic involving database transactions, concurrency control and race conditions.
3. AI symptom checker







dribbble.com


This feature integrates Google's Gemini API.

Example:

Patient enters:

Stomach pain + vomiting + fatigue

Functional flow:
1. React collects symptoms.
2. Backend sends the symptoms to Gemini API.
3. Gemini analyzes the input.
4. AI suggests a relevant medical specialization.
5. Application displays the recommendation.
For example, it might suggest consulting a gastroenterologist.

Interview concepts: REST API integration, external API calls, JSON processing and exception handling.

Important: AI suggestions are not a medical diagnosis.
4. Jeevanta Vault – Encrypted medical storage







sarthakroutray.github.io


This is one of the more interesting features of your project.

Patients can securely store:
- Medical reports.
- Prescriptions.
- Laboratory results.
- Other healthcare documents.
How it works:
1. Patient selects a document.
2. Application encrypts the document using AES.
3. Encrypted file is uploaded to Pinata IPFS.
4. The application retains the information needed to retrieve the file.
5. User provides the password to decrypt the document.
Technologies:
- AES encryption.
- Pinata.
- IPFS (InterPlanetary File System).
Interview concepts: Encryption, decentralized storage, data privacy and secure file handling.

Note: IPFS is decentralized file storage; using IPFS does not automatically make a document private. Encryption and secure key management are essential.
5. Medical report uploads







kindrahealth.app


Patients can upload medical reports while booking appointments.

Functional flow:
1. Patient selects a report.
2. React sends it to the backend.
3. Backend handles the file upload.
4. Cloudinary stores the file.
5. The associated file reference is used when retrieving the appointment information.
6. Doctor can access the uploaded report.
Interview concepts: Multipart requests, file handling, cloud storage and API integration.
6. Nearby therapist finder







webpt.com


This feature uses Google Maps integration.

Users can:
- Find nearby therapists.
- View their locations.
- Access contact information.
- Get directions.
Interview concepts: Google Maps API, geolocation and third-party integrations.
7. AI Telegram healthcare assistant







medium.com


This feature connects the healthcare application with Telegram.

Its functionality includes:
- Personalized healthcare plans.
- General wellness queries.
- Medication reminders.
- Notifications through Telegram.
Example:

A patient has a medication reminder scheduled for 8 PM.

The Telegram bot sends a notification at the scheduled time.

Interview concepts: Bot API integration, scheduled jobs, asynchronous processing and AI agents.

